# Base configuration.
#
# GoFr reads this file first, then overlays .$APP_ENV.env on top of it. The
# files beside this one therefore hold only what actually differs.
#
# Precedence, lowest to highest:
#
#   1. this file
#   2. .$APP_ENV.env  (or .local.env when APP_ENV is unset)
#   3. real environment variables - docker compose, systemd, the shell
#   4. configs/datasource.json, if the installer or the Settings screen has
#      written one
#
# The last two are why a deployment needs no file edits at all.
#
# APP_ENV itself can only come from the real environment - deploy/compose.yaml
# sets it to prod. GoFr reads it with os.Getenv and decides which overlay to
# open before it loads this file, so an APP_ENV written in here arrives too
# late to select anything and .local.env is used regardless.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# What is left here is what Settings cannot administer, in two kinds:
#
# BOOTSTRAP - settable only here or in the environment. These decide how the
# process starts, so an application that has not started cannot administer
# them; and getting one wrong must not be fixable only from a screen it takes
# away. AUTH_ENABLED switched off from the interface would open the instance to
# anyone, with no way back in.
#
# AT THE NEXT START - administered in the application, but stored rather than
# applied, because the framework reads them while starting up: the database
# connection, and the metrics and tracing settings below. What is stored wins
# over what is here, from the next start onwards.
#
# Deliberately absent: everything Settings administers while the application
# runs - the limits, the log level, the timezone and the LDAP connection. The
# limits and the log level did stand here once, as the values a fresh
# installation begins with. That made one value two places, which is one place
# that goes stale, and it was always the losing copy: what is stored wins, so
# the line here only ever described an instance nobody had touched yet.
#
# They are still named below, where they stood, with where they are set now -
# this file is also how somebody finds out which knobs exist. Removing them
# changed no behaviour: each held exactly the value the binary falls back to.

## App
# Stays, although Settings has an instance title, because the two are not the
# same thing. The title renames the browser tab and the header; this is the
# issuer an authenticator app shows beside a two-factor account, read once at
# start-up and administered by no screen. Changing it invalidates nothing -
# enrolled codes keep working - but every phone goes on showing the old name.
# A fresh installation also takes its title from here, so naming the instance
# in the environment does not have to be done a second time under Settings.
APP_NAME=Time Recording

# Deliberately not set here. GoFr reports APP_VERSION in the body of
# /.well-known/health, and the real version is stamped into the binary at build
# time - so a value in this file would be a constant that disagrees with the
# footer of the very interface it is serving. This file did ship 0.0.1 once,
# and every release then answered a monitoring check with 0.0.1 while the
# footer of the same instance correctly said the version that had been built.
# main.go exports the build's own version into the environment before GoFr
# starts, which is where GoFr looks for it.
#APP_VERSION=

# The API and the web interface. Not administrable, for the same reason as the
# metrics port below: the listener is bound at start-up.
#
# The container image used to set this as an environment variable as well,
# repeating the line - and an environment variable beats a configs directory
# mounted over the image's own, so it silently won against the operator's own
# file. The image now leaves the ports to this file. See the Dockerfile.
HTTP_PORT=8000

# How much the process writes. Set under Settings, in the telemetry card, and
# applied at the next start: the framework can change a logger's level, but it
# does so by assigning to a field every request goroutine reads without
# synchronisation.
#
# The levels are DEBUG, INFO, NOTICE, WARN, ERROR and FATAL. With none chosen
# the level is INFO, as is any other name: an unknown one is read as INFO
# rather than refused. .dev.env is the one file that still sets a level.
#LOG_LEVEL=

SHUTDOWN_GRACE_PERIOD=30s

# The Prometheus endpoint, on a port of its own. 0 switches it off.
#
# The port stays here rather than in the interface on purpose: the framework
# refuses to start when something already holds it, so a port that was free when
# it was saved and taken by the next start would stop the application - and the
# screen that could correct it with it. Settings can only switch the endpoint
# off, which cannot fail.
#
# Worth knowing before exposing it: this port asks for no password, is not
# covered by TLS, and serves Go's profiling endpoints under /debug/pprof/
# alongside the metrics.
METRICS_PORT=2121

## Database - bootstrap
# What everything else is stored in, including the settings the wizard writes.
#
# Deliberately empty. With a default here the application would silently come up
# on a throwaway SQLite file, someone would configure an installation on it -
# accounts, projects, recorded hours - and only discover which database they had
# been using when they tried to move to a real one. Switching then points the
# application at an empty database: the password change, the timezone and the
# instance title are all left behind, and start-up recreates the administrator
# with the initial password from the documentation.
#
# So with nothing configured the binary serves the installer instead of the
# application, and will not start until a connection has been chosen and proven
# to work. The installer writes it to configs/datasource.json, which overrides
# whatever is here from then on.
#
# Setting DB_DIALECT here or in the environment skips the installer, which is
# how a container deployment configures itself - see deploy/compose.yaml.
DB_DIALECT=
DB_NAME=

# Only used by the server dialects. DB_PORT can be left out: the application
# fills in the port the dialect is served on before GoFr reads any of this, so
# a PostgreSQL deployment that sets everything but the port does not end up
# dialling 3306 - which is GoFr's default for every dialect, not only MySQL.
# See config.DefaultPortFor.
# DB_HOST=localhost
# DB_PORT=5432
# DB_USER=postgres
# DB_PASSWORD=
# DB_SSL_MODE=disable
# DB_MAX_IDLE_CONNECTION=2
# DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNECTION=0

## Application - bootstrap
# Serve the embedded web interface. false runs the binary headless as an API,
# and removes the interface that would turn it back on.
UI_ENABLED=true

# Sign-in and role checks. false gives EVERY caller full administrative access.
# Only for a throwaway local trial, and never administrable from the interface.
AUTH_ENABLED=true

# On first start the built-in administrator admin@local is created with the
# password "changeme123". The setup wizard requires it to be changed before
# anything else can be used.

## Transport security - bootstrap
# The listener is bound at start-up, so a wrong value here makes the instance
# unreachable rather than merely wrong.
#
# TLS_DOMAINS is required when TLS_ENABLED is true and has no sensible default.
# Without it the application logs an error and continues on plain HTTP, so
# enabling TLS without naming a domain achieves nothing.
TLS_ENABLED=false
# TLS_DOMAINS=time.example.com
# TLS_EMAIL=ops@example.com

TLS_PORT=443
TLS_REDIRECT_PORT=80
TLS_CACHE_DIR=configs/certs

# Let's Encrypt's test authority: browsers do not trust those certificates, but
# the rate limits are far looser while setting things up.
TLS_STAGING=false

# How long browsers refuse plain HTTP for this host. Only ever sent over a
# connection that already is HTTPS - over plain HTTP it could lock a host out of
# working at all.
HSTS_MAX_AGE=8760h

## Scheduled jobs - bootstrap
# Cron jobs are registered once at start-up and cannot be re-registered while
# running, which is why the schedules stay here while their parameters do not.
# Empty disables the job.

# A synchronisation run DELETES accounts the directory no longer holds,
# together with their recorded time, private projects and tokens. That cannot be
# undone, so it is empty by default: an administrator can still start a run by
# hand, and the Settings screen offers a preview first.
# Administered under Settings as well, beside the buttons that run it by hand,
# where what is saved wins over this from the next start onwards. Not switchable
# while running: a scheduler is built while the application starts.
# LDAP_SYNC_SCHEDULE=0 4 * * *

## The limits - set under Settings, named here so they can be found
# The "operation and limits" card holds all of these. A change there takes
# effect within seconds without a restart, and what is stored wins over
# anything a file says, which is why none of them is written here any more.
# Left empty on that card, each falls back to the value built into the binary -
# the same value this file used to repeat, so a fresh installation begins where
# it always did. The card names what the instance is running with.
#
#   SESSION_LIFETIME            how long a sign-in lasts
#   SESSION_IDLE                how long a session may go unused before it
#                               ends anyway; empty or 0 for no such limit
#   MAX_DAILY_HOURS             the most one person may book on a single day
#   RATE_LIMIT                  requests one client may make
#   RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW           the window those are counted in
#   LDAP_SYNC_MAX_DELETE_RATIO  refuses a synchronisation that would remove
#                               more than this share of the directory-backed
#                               accounts - the guard against a truncated or
#                               misfiltered directory answer reading as a mass
#                               departure; 0 switches the check off

## Behind a reverse proxy
# Empty unless something else terminates the connection. Names the addresses
# whose X-Forwarded-For the rate limiter may believe, comma separated, as CIDR
# ranges or single addresses.
#
# Loopback is always believed and needs no entry here - the HTTPS front end in
# this binary reaches the plain port over it. Set this only for a proxy that is
# somewhere else, such as an nginx in the next container: without it every
# request through that proxy keys on the proxy, so the whole installation shares
# one budget and is locked out together the moment anybody is busy.
#
# Naming nothing is the safe default. The header is free for a client to write,
# so believed from the open network it hands out a fresh sign-in budget per
# request - which is the entire brute-force defence, since nothing locks an
# account after failed attempts.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=172.16.0.0/12

## Tracing (optional)
# Off unless an exporter is named. Administered under Settings as well, where
# what is saved wins over what is here from the next start onwards - so set it
# in one place or the other, not both.
#
# TRACE_EXPORTER is otlp or jaeger. TRACER_URL is the collector as host:port,
# without a scheme: the exporter speaks OTLP over gRPC, and a "http://" in front
# is read as part of the host name and resolves nothing. TRACER_RATIO is the
# share of traces recorded, between 0 and 1, and defaults to all of them.
#
# TRACE_EXPORTER=otlp
# TRACER_URL=jaeger:4317
# TRACER_RATIO=1
